WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
When a neuron needs a signal, which end must touch it?

2
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.600
Developing sympathetic neurons need nerve growth factor, or NGF, to survive.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.640
In an ordinary dish, one fluid bathes the cell body and its long axons.

4
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.140
So you cannot tell which part needs the factor.

5
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:27.067
In 1977, Robert Campenot divided one dish into chambers.

6
00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:32.847
A barrier sealed to the floor separates a central chamber from side chambers.

7
00:00:34.447 --> 00:00:39.007
Rat sympathetic cell bodies sit in the centre; their axons grow under the seal.

8
00:00:40.327 --> 00:00:44.327
Because the fluids stay apart, each chamber's bath can change on its own.

9
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.250
Now take NGF away from one side only.

10
00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.650
Keep it on the central cell bodies and in the right chamber.

11
00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.590
Drain it from the left chamber alone.

12
00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.230
What happens to the left branches, and to the same neuron's branches next door?

13
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.140
The left axons stop growing and later degenerate, though their cell bodies keep NGF.

14
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.340
The same neuron's branches next door stay: pruning in miniature, one branch cut

15
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.060
back. Now drain the factor from the centre alone.

16
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.420
When only the axons' far ends reach NGF, many cell bodies still survive.

17
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:34.113
Separating the two fluids reveals where a signal acts and whether it travels.

18
00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:40.493
Axons grow only where NGF touches them, whatever bathes the cell bodies.

19
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:46.593
Signaling proteins beside the receptor at the tip do some of that local work.

20
00:01:47.793 --> 00:01:50.093
Survival is the part that needs the nucleus.

21
00:01:51.313 --> 00:01:55.353
So when only the tips touch NGF, a signal must travel home.

22
00:01:56.417 --> 00:02:00.177
If the seal leaks, fluids mix and hide where a signal acts.

23
00:02:01.457 --> 00:02:04.697
Putting a factor straight on cell bodies bypasses the trip.

24
00:02:06.277 --> 00:02:09.957
Testing both sides separates local effects from signals sent home.

25
00:02:11.377 --> 00:02:16.717
Still, whether NGF itself travels, or a faster signal contributes, stays open.

26
00:02:17.957 --> 00:02:20.557
Nor is every arriving signal shown to be enough.

27
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:29.020
First, Campenot's 1977 dish separated local axon growth from survival signals sent

28
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:35.260
home. Second, Noo Li Jeon developed a small relative, the microfluidic chamber.

29
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:40.973
Natasha Sharma and colleagues in David Ginty's laboratory used it.

30
00:02:42.093 --> 00:02:47.673
Their axons got NGF; the receptor for it, TrkA, carried a tag called Flag.

31
00:02:48.913 --> 00:02:53.553
Over six hours, tagged TrkA appeared as dots in the dendrites, the receiving

32
00:02:53.553 --> 00:02:59.493
branches. That was compared with clustering of postsynaptic proteins, on the

33
00:02:59.493 --> 00:03:00.973
synapse's receiving side.

34
00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:07.087
Third, Phillip Nelson's laboratory used compartmented nerve-muscle cultures.

35
00:03:08.807 --> 00:03:15.547
Protein kinase C, or PKC, mediated heterosynaptic loss, where activity at one input

36
00:03:15.547 --> 00:03:17.267
costs its less active neighbour.

37
00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:23.007
Protein kinase A, or PKA, stabilized the stimulated input.

38
00:03:24.307 --> 00:03:28.247
Both acted through acetylcholine receptor numbers at the synapse.

39
00:03:29.347 --> 00:03:33.567
Dividing a neuron's bath shows where signals act and whether they travel.
